## 3. How Docker Fits into CI/CD

A typical container-based CI/CD workflow looks like this:

1. A developer commits code and opens a pull request.
2. CI runs validations, tests, and security checks.
3. CI builds a Docker image.
4. The image is tagged and pushed to a container registry.
5. CD deploys the image to an environment.
6. A container runtime starts the container.
7. Persistent data is stored using appropriate volumes or external storage.

Example:

```text
Developer
    |
    v
Git Repository
    |
    v
CI Pipeline
    |
    +--> Build
    +--> Test
    +--> Security Scans
    |
    v
Container Image
    |
    v
Container Registry
    |
    v
CD Pipeline
    |
    v
Deployment Environment
    |
    +--> Docker on a supported server
    |
    +--> Kubernetes cluster
    |
    +--> AWS ECS
```

**Key principle:** Building an image and running an image are different activities.

The deployment target needs a compatible runtime or orchestration environment. The CI/CD runner does not necessarily need to be the same machine that runs the application.

Kubernetes orchestrates containers across nodes. Its nodes use a compatible container runtime, commonly containerd, to run them.

