## 9. IIS, Containers, and AWS Hosting Options

There is no need to migrate immediately to Kubernetes.

| Option | When it may fit |
|---|---|
| IIS on EC2 | Existing Windows/.NET application that already runs reliably on IIS. |
| Docker on a supported server | Useful when packaging and running the application as a container offers operational benefits. |
| Amazon ECS | A managed container-orchestration option if RAD365 benefits from container-based deployment. |
| Amazon EKS/Kubernetes | Useful when Kubernetes capabilities justify the additional operational complexity. |
| .NET on Linux with Kestrel | A potential option for compatible applications, depending on framework and dependency support. |

**Initial recommendation:** Automate the existing IIS deployment first. Evaluate containers only after understanding the application framework, dependencies, and operational requirements.

If RAD365 uses older ASP.NET Framework components, Windows/IIS may remain the most practical option. If it uses modern ASP.NET Core, more hosting options may be available.

